<h1>🪨 Geodes &amp; Nodules</h1>
<div class="collection-intro">
<p class="lead">A geode is a rock with a hollow, crystal-lined cavity. A nodule is the same kind of structure filled solid, often with agate. A thunderegg is a nodule that grew inside volcanic rhyolite.</p>
</div>
<div class="formation-grid">
  <div><h3>1 · A cavity forms</h3><p>Gas bubbles in cooling lava, dissolved pockets in limestone, or hollows left by roots, shells or concretions in sediment make an empty space.</p></div>
  <div><h3>2 · A rind hardens</h3><p>Silica-rich fluids coat the cavity wall with a tough skin of chalcedony. This is why geodes weather out of softer rock as round "rocks".</p></div>
  <div><h3>3 · Crystals grow inward</h3><p>Over thousands to millions of years, mineral-rich groundwater seeps in. Quartz, amethyst, calcite, celestite or other minerals crystallize from the wall toward the centre.</p></div>
  <div><h3>Geode, nodule or thunderegg?</h3><p>If the space stays partly open, it is a <strong>geode</strong>. If it fills completely, often with banded agate, it is a <strong>nodule</strong>. A <strong>thunderegg</strong> forms in rhyolite and usually has a star-shaped agate core.</p></div>
</div>
